"}},{"id":"DpBE49T1RVnt9FxqA5fsh","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"","caption":"Carey Creek Ramp","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"105333","photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/DpBE49T1RVnt9FxqA5fsh","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/DpBE49T1RVnt9FxqA5fsh"}},{"id":"0vsGHGSHWUtOeWZdYwgCd","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"","caption":"Approaching Carey Creek Ramp","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"105333","photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/0vsGHGSHWUtOeWZdYwgCd","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/0vsGHGSHWUtOeWZdYwgCd"}},{"id":"UtslkRA4iy1qgvpC4s7hv","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"The Crevice, looking downstream from the Manning Bridge. This was once a proposed site for an Army Corps of Engineers dam project that would have flooded the river 70 miles upstream from this site.","caption":"The Crevice","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/UtslkRA4iy1qgvpC4s7hv","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/UtslkRA4iy1qgvpC4s7hv"}},{"id":"JrJGw2dvhMAeAU0TCAeqg","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"Running Recognized by the yellow Lake Creek Bridge. The rapids are just upstream of the bridge.","caption":"Raft Entering Lake Creek Rapids","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"105339","photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/JrJGw2dvhMAeAU0TCAeqg","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/JrJGw2dvhMAeAU0TCAeqg"}},{"id":"z49LgQraT5nLI8Wrmt9hh","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"","caption":"Carey Creek Ramp","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"105333","photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/z49LgQraT5nLI8Wrmt9hh","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/z49LgQraT5nLI8Wrmt9hh"}},{"id":"0Tw7cKvXGkZ0q12VZcHsS","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"View from the road looking downstream towards Flock Canyon and the Elkhorn beaches on river left from USGS River Mile 101 to 102.","caption":"Looking Downstream Towards Flock Canyon","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/0Tw7cKvXGkZ0q12VZcHsS","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/0Tw7cKvXGkZ0q12VZcHsS"}},{"id":"I90CJoxXXpyDEbrAn9j4n","ext":"jpg","post_id":"FrbWVRtnEzJ7QXPMcHMCf","description":"A long paved ramp, designed to accommodate a wide range of flow levels at the Riggins Town Park.","caption":"Concrete Ramp at Riggins Town Park","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"116637","photo_date":"2019-06-12T15:58:21.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-08T15:58:21.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/I90CJoxXXpyDEbrAn9j4n","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/I90CJoxXXpyDEbrAn9j4n"}},{"id":"9ra0heh0s5LIbAUW69FhF","ext":"jpg","post_id":"FrbWVRtnEzJ7QXPMcHMCf","description":"The paved parking area on a bench high above the river.","caption":"Parking Area in the Riggins Park","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"116637","photo_date":"2019-06-12T15:58:21.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-08T15:58:21.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9ra0heh0s5LIbAUW69FhF","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9ra0heh0s5LIbAUW69FhF"}},{"id":"pt5cDUnUe958vYDDtdcuL","ext":"jpg","post_id":"VFYcTjZbQLpsfASVFv8Ul","description":"The view downstream on the Salmon River at Chamberlain Gulch.","caption":"Downstream View at Chamberlain Gulch","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-08-30T19:01:20.000Z","updated_at":"2022-11-17T19:01:20.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/pt5cDUnUe958vYDDtdcuL","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/pt5cDUnUe958vYDDtdcuL"}},{"id":"KOpdbXeo3NKWsaSMB9bmq","ext":"jpg","post_id":"FrbWVRtnEzJ7QXPMcHMCf","description":"Replica of a Scow, a large wooden sweep boat as used by Captain Guleke to make float trips down the Salmon River. Beginning in the late 1890's Cap would build these boats for the one-way trip down the river to deliver supplies and then sell the boat for the value of the lumber once he completed the trip. Cap would also take passengers down and these trips continued into the 1930's.","caption":"Replica of a Scow","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"116637","photo_date":"2019-06-12T15:58:21.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-08T15:58:21.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/KOpdbXeo3NKWsaSMB9bmq","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/KOpdbXeo3NKWsaSMB9bmq"}},{"id":"EjzfMLijobFL9FScMv01U","ext":"jpg","post_id":"FrbWVRtnEzJ7QXPMcHMCf","description":"A nice lawn and picnic area in the park in Riggins.","caption":"Lawn in the Riggins Park","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"116637","photo_date":"2019-06-12T15:58:21.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-08T15:58:21.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/EjzfMLijobFL9FScMv01U","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/EjzfMLijobFL9FScMv01U"}}],"detail":{"id":"1464","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is a wonderful section of river immediately downstream of the more well-known Main Salmon which is the classic multi-day trip. This river segment flows for 28 miles among pine forests and granite walls with the Salmon River Road (Forest Road 1614) paralleling the river. It provides great opportunities for day trips with easy logistics and good camping (either in campgrounds or on one of the many sandy beaches) with no permits are required.\n\nWhile the high flows of spring can create hazardous conditions (experts only), mid-summer brings more moderate flows and classic pool-drop rapids with good flatwater recovery sections in between. Some nice beaches provide places to hang out for lunch or engage in water play.\n\nLocal outfitters provide options for less experienced paddlers who have interest in a day trip (check with outfitters in Riggins).\n\nLogistics:\n\nA Salmon River Road (Forest Road 1614) offers multiple access points. In addition to the formal sites below there are a few beaches that can be accessed from the road and are enjoyed when they emerge during the later half of the summer.\n\nThe turn for the Salmon River Road (Forest Road 1614) is at Highway 95 mile 194.4 at the south end of town in Riggins. The road heads east for 26 miles where it ends at Vinegar Creek. Access points and features along the way include:\n\nMile 1.4, Shorts Bar: This primitive campground (no fee) and boat launch is administered by the Bureau of Land Management.\n\nMile 2.3, road becomes single lane\n\nMile 4.0, Island Bar: This primitive campground (no fee) and boat launch is administered by the Bureau of Land Management.\n\nMile 6.6, Lake Creek Bridge (aka Yellow Bridge)\n\nMile 9.8, Allison Creek Picnic Area: Picnic site along the Salmon River administered by the Forest Service.\n\nMile 10.5, Spring Bar Campground: This fee campground administered by the Forest Service offers a boat launching ramp and 18 tent campsites that are open year around. A table and fire ring are provided at all but two of the sites. Campsites are available on a first-come, first-serve basis.\n\nMile 11.3, Van Creek Campground: primitive camping area with toilet administered by the Forest Service.\n\nMile 12.7, pavement ends\n\nMile 13.6, Manning Bridge: river crossing originally constructed by the CCC with the new bridge constructed in 2018.\n\nMile 23.4, Carey Creek River Access: This is a popular access point for rafters and kayakers with a paved ramp that provides easy access to the river. The site has picnic tables and toilets.\n\nMile 26.0 Vinegar Creek River Access: End of road site and heavily used by jet boaters. Site has a boat launching ramp, but is not recommended for average user (4-wheel drive and winch required to get back up the ramp). Toilets are available here.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"23","correlations":[{"reachID":"1464","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"1000","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"100000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"SALMON RIVER AT WHITE BIRD ID","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"13317000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"37600","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"22